---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const description = "A short picture of how GPUfit decides whether a model fits, and how fast it might run.";
---

<Base title="How GPUfit estimates fit and speed" description={description} path="/how-it-works">
  <article class="wrap" style="padding:2rem 0 1rem; max-width:52rem;">
    <p class="kicker">Method</p>
    <h1 class="serif" style="font-size:clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); margin-bottom:0.4rem;">How it works</h1>
    <p class="muted" style="max-width:40rem;">Every number is an estimate for llama.cpp, not a benchmark. Open a section to see the picture, then the short explanation.</p>

    <div class="method-details">
      <details open>
        <summary>Memory: weights, conversation, and a little spare room</summary>
        <div class="method-grid three" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <section class="figure-card">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 280 150" role="img" aria-label="A large weights block beside a thinner KV block">
              <rect x="16" y="18" width="248" height="114" rx="16" fill="none" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" />
              <rect x="32" y="36" width="168" height="78" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.9" />
              <rect x="206" y="36" width="42" height="78" rx="8" fill="var(--tight)" opacity="0.85" />
              <text x="116" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-size="14" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Weights</text>
              <text x="227" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--bg)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">KV</text>
            </svg>
            <h2 class="serif">Weights</h2>
            <p class="muted">The GGUF file. A smaller quant is a smaller file, and a slightly less faithful model.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="figure-card">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 280 150" role="img" aria-label="Bars growing from 2K to 128K of context">
              <rect x="24" y="108" width="36" height="18" rx="4" fill="var(--tight)" />
              <rect x="70" y="86" width="36" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--tight)" />
              <rect x="116" y="58" width="36" height="68" rx="4" fill="var(--tight)" />
              <rect x="162" y="30" width="36" height="96" rx="4" fill="var(--off)" />
              <text x="42" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">2K</text>
              <text x="88" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">8K</text>
              <text x="134" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">32K</text>
              <text x="180" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">128K</text>
            </svg>
            <h2 class="serif">KV cache</h2>
            <p class="muted">The conversation’s memory. A model that fits at 8K can miss at 128K.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="figure-card">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 280 150" role="img" aria-label="A memory bar with a small reserved end">
              <rect x="20" y="48" width="240" height="36" rx="18" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--line)" />
              <rect x="24" y="52" width="176" height="28" rx="14" fill="var(--accent)" />
              <rect x="204" y="52" width="28" height="28" rx="8" fill="var(--tight)" />
              <rect x="236" y="52" width="20" height="28" rx="8" fill="var(--line)" />
              <text x="112" y="71" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-size="12" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">usable</text>
              <text x="20" y="112" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Display and a little headroom stay free.</text>
            </svg>
            <h2 class="serif">Overhead</h2>
            <p class="muted">llama.cpp needs a little extra. A card driving a monitor cannot give you every byte.</p>
          </section>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Total memory is weights + KV cache + engine overhead. Real GGUF file sizes win. A file smaller than about 40% of parameters × bits-per-weight / 8 is treated as a stray shard and ignored. Q4_K_M is about 4.85 bits per weight.</p>
          <p>Ordinary KV cache, in FP16, is <code>2 × layers × KV heads × head dim × 2 × context</code>. Sliding-window layers only keep the window. DeepSeek-style MLA stores a short latent. Linear and Mamba layers keep a fixed state.</p>
          <p>llama.cpp overhead is 0.3 GB plus 1% of the weights. A discrete GPU that drives a monitor loses 0.5 GB, then 5% more. Apple unified memory, and parts like Ryzen AI Max, budget 75% of RAM for the GPU and always leave about 5 GB for the OS. CPU-only runs keep 4 GB plus 10%.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details open>
        <summary>The verdict</summary>
        <section class="figure-card" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 520 90" role="img" aria-label="A meter marked runs great, runs, tight fit, and won't fit">
            <rect x="16" y="16" width="488" height="28" rx="14" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--line)" />
            <rect x="18" y="18" width="280" height="24" rx="12" fill="var(--great)" />
            <rect x="298" y="18" width="110" height="24" fill="var(--runs)" />
            <rect x="408" y="18" width="60" height="24" fill="var(--tight)" />
            <rect x="468" y="18" width="34" height="24" rx="12" fill="var(--bad)" />
            <text x="150" y="35" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-size="12" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Runs great</text>
            <text x="350" y="35" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--bg)" font-size="12" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Runs</text>
            <text x="438" y="35" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--bg)" font-size="11" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Tight</text>
            <text x="16" y="72" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="13" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">Under 60% feels comfortable. Past 98%, it does not fit.</text>
          </svg>
        </section>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>We pick the best quant that still leaves room for a normal chat. Runs great is at or under 60% of the usable pool, Runs is 85%, and Tight fit is 98%. If the whole file will not fit, some layers can sit in system RAM. That is slower, and the page says so.</p>
          <p>The home page leads with models that fit fully at about 10 tokens per second or faster, largest first, with a discount when the only quant that fits is well below Q4. The three cards are the best general-chat, coding, and reasoning matches.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details open>
        <summary>The speed</summary>
        <section class="figure-card" style="margin-top:1rem; max-width:24rem;">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 280 110" role="img" aria-label="Memory bandwidth pouring into a token">
            <rect x="16" y="28" width="90" height="48" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" />
            <text x="61" y="57" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-size="13" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">RAM</text>
            <path d="M112 52h70" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
            <path d="M170 44l16 8-16 8" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            <circle cx="220" cy="52" r="28" fill="none" stroke="var(--tight)" stroke-width="3" />
            <text x="220" y="57" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-size="13" font-family="Outfit, sans-serif">tok</text>
          </svg>
        </section>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Each token rereads the weights. A wider memory bus finishes that read sooner. The range is 55–75% of the card’s peak, not a benchmark. Treat “20–30 tok/s” as a ceiling, not a promise. Prefill, speculative decoding, KV-cache quantization, and multi-GPU are not modeled.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Where the numbers come from</summary>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Model sizes and configs come from the public Hugging Face API. Hardware is a hand-checked table, one source link per row, including NVIDIA’s compare page and Apple’s specs. Ollama commands use the public library name when it matches. A job refreshes the catalog every six hours and drops fine-tunes and duplicate variants.</p>
          <p>Nothing here is fitted to llama-bench yet. A speed you type on the home page stays in this browser.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
